gwan Shri Nakoda Parshwanath, 21 inches tall and 18 inches wide, in the padmasana posture. The image is adorned with a canopy of 7 serpent hoods.. Location: This is 13 kilometers from Balotra, deep in the forest amid hills.. History: Two sons of a king, named Veeramdatt and Nakorsen, built two cities 10 miles apart, named Veerampur and Nakornagar. These kings built temples in their respective cities. An image of Shri Chandraprabhu Swami was installed in Veerampur, and an image of Shri Suparshwanath Swami was installed in Nakorsen in the third century of the Vikram era, consecrated by the auspicious hands of the venerable Acharya Shalibhadraswami. Over time, this temple was renovated several times. In V.S. 909, the city of Veerampur was thriving, home to about 2,700 prosperous Shravak households. At that time, Shreshthi Shri Harakhchandji renovated the ancient temple and had an image of Bhagwan Mahavir consecrated there. According to another tradition, this image appeared from a pond near Sindri village, close to Nakornagar, in response to a sign given in a dream by the presiding deity to the fortunate Shravak Shri Jindatt. There is a record of a further renovation in V.S. 1223. In V.S. 1280 Alamshah attacked this city, causing heavy damage to the temple; at that time the Sangh hid these 120 images for safekeeping in an underground chamber at Kaliddrah village. At the start of the 15th century, work began again to rebuild this temple, and the 120 ancient images of Nakornagar kept at Kaliddrah were brought here, and among them this beautiful, miraculous image of Bhagwan Parshwanath was once again installed as the Mulanayak in this newly built temple in V.S. 1429, where it remains to this day. Because the original image was from Nakornagar, this pilgrimage site came to be known as \\\"Nakoda Parshwanath.\\\" The image of the manifestly powerful presiding deity Bhairavji was installed here by Acharya Kirtiratnasurishwarji. Bhairavji is very miraculous; he protects the pilgrimage site and fulfills the wishes of worshippers, and his miracles are known throughout the world. There is a record of a renovation carried out in V.S. 1564 by Seth Sadrang, great-grandson of Seth Juthil of the Oswal Vanshahaj gotra. There is also a record of a renovation of this temple in V.S. 1638. The prosperity of this place remained good until about the 17th century. After that, Shreshthi Malashah Sanklecha's brother Nanakji, seeing the unpleasant conduct of the local prince, decided to leave the village; so the Sangh of Jaisalmer left the village along with all the Jain families. After that the population of this village steadily declined. Today there is not a single Jain household here, but the arrangements for the pilgrimage site maintained by the Shri Sangh are noteworthy. After the 17th century, there is a record of a renovation in Samvat 1886, and necessary renovations have continued to take place from time to time since. Apart from the temple there are no houses here, but because of the constant flow of pilgrims, the place has the feel of a small town. Every year on Paush Krishna Dashami, the birth anniversary (Janma Kalyanak) of Shri Parshwa Prabhu, a large fair is held.. Other Temples: There are also temples of Bhagwan Shantinath and Bhagwan Adishwar, believed to have been built in the 17th century.. Art and Sculpture: Images dating from the 12th to the 16th centuries are on display here. The natural scenery of this secluded spot amid the hills in the forest is very beautiful, peaceful and captivating. The image of Bhagwan Parshwanath is deeply moving. Among the pilgrimage sites of Rajasthan, this tirtha holds special importance.. Directions: The nearest railway station, Balotra, is 13 kilometers from this temple. Bus service and private vehicles are available to this place from every pilgrimage site in Rajasthan. There are dharamshalas with excellent accommodation within the temple complex. There is also an ayambil hall, a bhojanshala, an upashraya, a gyan bhandar (library), and a panjrapole.. Scripture References: This temple is mentioned in \\\"Tirthamala,\\\" \\\"Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,\\\" and elsewhere. There is an image of Shri Nakoda Parshwanath at the \\\"Kalikund Parshwanath Temple\\\" in Santacruz, Mumbai, and at \\\"Jiravala Tirth.\\\". Hymn of Praise: From whose face nectar first flows, whose eyes stream with the essence of ambrosia, the King of Mewanagar who fulfills the hopes of all who behold him, with Nakoda Bhairav's protection ever at hand -- \\\"I bow with devotion to Shri Nakoda Parshwa.\\\". Chanting Mantra: \\\"Om Hrim Arham Shri Nakoda Parshwanathay Namah.\\\". Trust: Shri Jain Shwetambar Nakoda Parshwanath Tirth, Post: Mewanagar, Balotra: 344025, District: Barmer, Rajasthan, India. Phone: 02988-40761, 02988-40005, 02988-40096. Fax: 02988-40762. Mobile: 6350124634.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"a\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"https://goo.gl/maps/6h8cbcZNdpgLsbyr7\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"_blank\\\",\\\"rel\\\":\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-maroon px-5 py-2.5 font-ui text-sm font-medium text-ivory hover:bg-maroon-deep transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":\\\"$8:2:props:children:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:iconNode\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-map-pin h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],\\\"Open in Google Maps\\\"]}],null,[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L20\\\",null,{\\\"text\\\":\\\"$21\\\",\\\"lang\\\":\\\"en\\\",\\\"label\\\":\\\"Listen\\\",\\\"stopLabel\\\":\\\"Stop\\\"}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1c:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-10 grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 sm:grid-cols-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"1babeb6d-8152-4b65-9